{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1456 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ad732b65125b4bbdf58da50ae60dbb5a2d04978d0cc8f56cab1f8c56209d93cd" :edb/db-before-t 1455 :edb/db-after-t 1456 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534768 50 #inst "2026-10-08T06:23:36.591Z" 13194139534768 true] [17592186133605 1100 "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n$\\langle {x(t)}, {y(t)} \\rangle = x(0)y(0) + x(1)y(1) + x(-1)y(-1)$.\nGiven that $p(t) = 1 + t + t^2$ and $q(t) = kt - t^2$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$, find the value of $k$." 13194139534768 false] [17592186133605 1100 "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle {x(t)}, {y(t)} \\rangle   = x(0)y(0) + x(1)y(1) + x(-1)y(-1).\n$$\n\nGiven that $p(t) = 1 + t + t^2$ and $q(t) = kt - t^2$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" 13194139534768 true] [17592186133605 1145 "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨p(t), q(t)⟩ &= p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1) + p(-1)q(-1) \\\\\n&= (1)(0) + (3)(k - 1) + (1)(-k - 1) \\\\\n&= 0 + (3k - 3) + (-k - 1) \\\\\n&= 2k - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set\n\n$$\n\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nand solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨p(t), q(t)⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n2k - 4 &= 0 \\\\\n2k &= 4 \\\\\nk &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534768 false] [17592186133605 1145 "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)+p(-1)q(-1) \\\\  & =(1)(0)+(3)(k-1)+(1)(-k-1) \\\\  & =0+(3k-3)+(-k-1) \\\\  & =2k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2k-4 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534768 true] [17592186133606 1100 "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right]$, equipped with the inner product\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t$.\nGiven that $x(t) = kt^2+3t$ and $y(t) = t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$, find the value of $k$." 13194139534768 false] [17592186133606 1100 "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x(t) = kt^2+3t$ and $y(t) = t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" 13194139534768 true] [17592186133606 1145 "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ &= ∫_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(kt^{2} + 3t)(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(kt^{3} + 3t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= (\\frac{kt^{4}}{4} + t^{3}) \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{4} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nand solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{k}{4} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{k}{4} &= -1 \\\\\nk &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534768 false] [17592186133606 1145 "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{2}+3t)(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{4}}{4}+{t}^{3}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{4}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{4}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{4} & =-1 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534768 true] [17592186133607 1100 "Let $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2 x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nGiven that $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" 13194139534768 true] [17592186133607 1100 "Let $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n$\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2 x_3 y_3$.\n\nGiven that $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$, find the value of $k$." 13194139534768 false] [17592186133607 1145 "Since $\\mathbf x$ and $\\mathbf y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y⟩ &= 3x_{1}y_{1} + x_{2}y_{2} + 2x_{3}y_{3} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot (2 \\cdot k) + 1 \\cdot (-2) + 2 \\cdot (2 \\cdot (-1)) \\\\\n&= 6k - 2 - 4 \\\\\n&= 6k - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nand solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n6k - 6 &= 0 \\\\\n6k &= 6 \\\\\nk &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534768 false] [17592186133607 1145 "Since $\\mathbf x$ and $\\mathbf y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2\\cdot k)+1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =6k-2-4 \\\\  & =6k-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6k-6 & =0 \\\\ 6k & =6 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534768 true] [17592186133608 1100 "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,2\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,2\\pi\\right]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$\\langle f(t),g(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(t) g(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t$.\n\nGiven the functions $f(t) = \\cos t$ and $g(t)=\\sin 2t$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = 0$\n2. $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n3. $f(t) \\perp g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity* $\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta=\\dfrac12(\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta))$." 13194139534768 false] [17592186133608 1100 "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,2\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,2\\pi\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle f(t),g(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(t) g(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t) = \\cos t$ and $g(t)=\\sin 2t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = 0$\n\n- $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $f(t) \\perp g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity* $\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta=\\dfrac12(\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)).$" 13194139534768 true] [17592186133608 1145 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle  =  \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\cos{t} \\sin2t \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{3}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-1-\\frac{1}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-1-\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3})-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534768 true] [17592186133608 1145 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\cos{t} \\sin2t \\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2π}\\sin 2t\\cos tdt &= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{2π}(\\sin (2t - t) + \\sin (2t + t))dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{2π}(\\sin t + \\sin 3t)dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(-\\cos t - \\frac{\\cos 3t}{3}) \\mid _{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}[- \\cos (2π) - (\\cos (6π))/(3)] - \\frac{1}{2}[- \\cos 0 - \\frac{\\cos 0}{3}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}[- 1 - \\frac{1}{3}] - \\frac{1}{2}[- 1 - \\frac{1}{3}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false.\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534768 false] [17592186133789 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308870 13194139534768 true] [17592186133789 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308877 13194139534768 true] [17592186133789 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308933 13194139534768 true] [17592186133789 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308940 13194139534768 true] [17592186133790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308884 13194139534768 true] [17592186133790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308905 13194139534768 true] [17592186133790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308947 13194139534768 true] [17592186133790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308954 13194139534768 true] [17592186133791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308863 13194139534768 true] [17592186133791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308912 13194139534768 true] [17592186133791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308919 13194139534768 true] [17592186133791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308926 13194139534768 true] [17592186133792 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308891 13194139534768 true] [17592186133792 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308898 13194139534768 true] [17592186133792 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308961 13194139534768 true] [17592186133792 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308968 13194139534768 true] [17592186133792 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308975 13194139534768 true] [17592186134358 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534768 true] [17592186134358 1100 "Let $V = C[0, 1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0, 1]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = ∫_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)dt$.\nIf $x(t) = t$ and $y(t) = t^{2} + k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ in $V$, then $k =$" 13194139534768 false] [17592186134358 1100 "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=t$ and $y(t)={t}^{2}+k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" 13194139534768 true] [17592186134358 1145 "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t({t}^{2}+k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}+kt)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{k}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{k}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{2} & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534768 true] [17592186134360 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534768 true] [17592186134360 1100 "Let $V = R^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨x, y⟩ = x_{1}y_{1} + 2x_{2}y_{2} + 3x_{3}y_{3}$.\nIf $x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ in $V$, then $k =$" 13194139534768 false] [17592186134360 1100 "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" 13194139534768 true] [17592186134360 1145 "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-1\\cdot k)+3\\cdot (2\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-2-2k+18 \\\\  & =16-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 16-2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =16 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 131941395347